iable interpolation is done with `#var` (similar to `$var` in"r" `macro_rules!` macros). This grabs the `var` variable that is currently in"r" scope and inserts it in that location in the output tokens. Any type"r" implementing the [`ToTokens`] trait can be interpolated. This includes most"r" Rust primitive types as well as most of the syntax tree types from the [Syn]"r" crate."r" [Syn]: https://github.com/dtolnay/syn"r" Repetition is done using `#(...)*` or `#(...),*` again similar to"r" `macro_rules!`. This iterates through the elements of any variable"r" interpolated within the repetition and inserts a copy of the repetition body"r" for each one. The variables in an interpolation may be a `Vec`, slice,"r" `BTreeSet`, or any `Iterator`."r" - `#(#var)*` — no separators"r" - `#(#var),*` — the character before the asterisk is used as a separator"r" - `#( struct #var; )*` — the repetition can contain other tokens"r#" - `#( #k => println!("{}", #v), )*` — even multiple interpolations"#r" # Hygiene"r" Any interpolated tokens preserve the `Span` information provided by their"r" `ToTokens` implementation. Tokens that originate within the `quote!`"r" invocation are spanned with [`Span::call_site()`]."r" [`Span::call_site()`]: proc_macro2::Span::call_site"r" A different span can be provided through the [`quote_spanned!`] macro."r" # Return type"r" The macro evaluates to an expression of type `proc_macro2::TokenStream`."r" Meanwhile Rust procedural macros are expected to return the type"r" `proc_macro::TokenStream`."r" The difference between the two types is that `proc_macro` types are entirely"r" specific to procedural macros and cannot ever exist in code outside of a"r" procedural macro, while `proc_macro2` types may exist anywhere including"r" tests and non-macro code like main.rs and build.rs. This is why even the"r" procedural macro ecosystem is largely built around `proc_macro2`, because"r" that ensures the libraries are unit testable and accessible in non-macro"r" contexts."r" There is a [`From`]-conversion in both directions so returning the output of"r" `quote!` from a procedural macro usually looks like `tokens.into()` or"r" `proc_macro::TokenStream::from(tokens)`."r" # Examples"r" ### Procedural macro"r" The structure of a basic procedural macro is as follows. Refer to the [Syn]"r" crate for further useful guidance on using `quote!` as part of a procedural"r" macro."r" # #[cfg(any())]"r" extern crate proc_macro;"r" # extern crate proc_macro2;"r" use proc_macro::TokenStream;"r" # use proc_macro2::TokenStream;"r" use quote::quote;"r" # const IGNORE_TOKENS: &'static str = stringify! {"r" #[proc_macro_derive(HeapSize)]"r" # };"r" pub fn derive_heap_size(input: TokenStream) -> TokenStream {"r"     // Parse the input and figure out what implementation to generate..."r"     # const IGNORE_TOKENS: &'static str = stringify! {"r"     let name = /* ... */;"r"     let expr = /* ... */;"r"     # };"r"     #"r"     # let name = 0;"r"     # let expr = 0;"r"     let expanded = quote! {"r"         // The generated impl."r"         impl heapsize::HeapSize for #name {"r"             fn heap_size_of_children(&self) -> usize {"r"                 #expr"r"             }"r"         }"r"     };"r"     // Hand the output tokens back to the compiler."r"     TokenStream::from(expanded)"r" <p><br></p>"r" ### Combining quoted fragments"r" Usually you don't end up constructing an entire final `TokenStream` in one"r" piece. Different parts may come from different helper functions. The tokens"r" produced by `quote!` themselves implement `ToTokens` and so can be"r" interpolated into later `quote!` invocations to build up a final result."r" # use quote::quote;"r" let type_definition = quote! {...};"r" let methods = quote! {...};"r" let tokens = quote! {"r"     #type_definition"r"     #methods"r" };"r" ### Constructing identifiers"r" Suppose we have an identifier `ident` which came from somewhere in a macro"r" input and we need to modify it in some way for the macro output. Let's"r" consider prepending the identifier with an underscore."r" Simply interpolating the identifier next to an underscore will not have the"r" behavior of concatenating them. The underscore and the identifier will"r" continue to be two separate tokens as if you had written `_ x`."r" ```edition2018"r" # use proc_macro2::{self as syn, Span};"r#" # let ident = syn::Ident::new("i", Span::call_site());"#r" // incorrect"r" quote! {"r"     let mut _#ident = 0;"r" # ;"r" The solution is to build a new identifier token with the correct value. As"r" this is such a common case, the [`format_ident!`] macro provides a"r" convenient utility for doing so correctly."r" # use proc_macro2::{Ident, Span};"r" # use quote::{format_ident, quote};"r#" # let ident = Ident::new("i", Span::call_site());"#r#" let varname = format_ident!("_{}", ident);"#r"     let mut #varname = 0;"r" Alternatively, the APIs provided by Syn and proc-macro2 can be used to"r" directly build the identifier. This is roughly equivalent to the above, but"r" will not handle `ident` being a raw identifier."r#" let concatenated = format!("_{}", ident);"#r" let varname = syn::Ident::new(&concatenated, ident.span());"r" ### Making method calls"r" Let's say our macro requires some type specified in the macro input to have"r" a constructor called `new`. We have the type in a variable called"r" `field_type` of type `syn::Type` and want to invoke the constructor."r" # let field_type = quote!(...);"r"     let value = #field_type::new();"r" This works only sometimes. If `field_type` is `String`, the expanded code"r" contains `String::new()` which is fine. But if `field_type` is something"r" like `Vec<i32>` then the expanded code is `Vec<i32>::new()` which is invalid"r" syntax. Ordinarily in handwritten Rust we would write `Vec::<i32>::new()`"r" but for macros often the following is more convenient."r"     let value = <#field_type>::new();"r" This expands to `<Vec<i32>>::new()` which behaves correctly."r" A similar pattern is appropriate for trait methods."r"     let value = <#field_type as core::default::Default>::default();"r" ### Interpolating text inside of doc comments"r" Neither doc comments nor string literals get interpolation behavior in"r" quote:"r" ```compile_fail"r"     /// try to interpolate: #ident"r"     ///"r"     /// ..."r#"     #[doc = "try to interpolate: #ident"]"#r" Instead the best way to build doc comments that involve variables is by"r" formatting the doc string literal outside of quote."r" ```rust"r" # const IGNORE: &str = stringify! {"r" let msg = format!(...);"r#" # let ident = Ident::new("var", Span::call_site());"#r#" # let msg = format!("try to interpolate: {}", ident);"#r"     #[doc = #msg]"r" ### Indexing into a tuple struct"r" When interpolating indices of a tuple or tuple struct, we need them not to"r" appears suffixed as integer literals by interpolating them as [`syn::Index`]"r" instead."r" [`syn::Index`]: https://docs.rs/syn/2.0/syn/struct.Index.html"r" let i = 0usize..self.fields.len();"r" // expands to 0 + self.0usize.heap_size() + self.1usize.heap_size() + ..."r" // which is not valid syntax"r"     0 #( + self.#i.heap_size() )*"r" # use proc_macro2::{Ident, TokenStream};"r" # mod syn {"r" #     use proc_macro2::{Literal, TokenStream};"r" #     use quote::{ToTokens, TokenStreamExt};"r" #     pub struct Index(usize);"r" #     impl From<usize> for Index {"r" #         fn from(i: usize) -> Self {"r" #             Index(i)"r" #         }"r" #     }"r" #     impl ToTokens for Index {"r" #         fn to_tokens(&self, tokens: &mut TokenStream) {"r" #             tokens.append(Literal::usize_unsuffixed(self.0));"r" # struct Struct {"r" #     fields: Vec<Ident>,"r" # impl Struct {"r" #     fn example(&self) -> TokenStream {"r" let i = (0..self.fields.len()).map(syn::Index::from);"r" // expands to 0 + self.0.heap_size() + self.1.heap_size() + ..."__quote_spannedr" Same as `quote!`, but applies a given span to all tokens originating within"r" the macro invocation."r" # Syntax"r" A span expression of type [`Span`], followed by `=>`, followed by the tokens"r" to quote. The span expression should be brief &mdash; use a variable for"r" anything more than a few characters. There should be no space before the"r" `=>` token."r" [`Span`]: proc_macro2::Span"r" # use proc_macro2::Span;"r" # use quote::quote_spanned;"r" let span = /* ... */;"r" # let span = Span::call_site();"r" # let init = 0;"r" // On one line, use parentheses."r" let tokens = quote_spanned!(span=> Box::into_raw(Box::new(#init)));"r" // On multiple lines, place the span at the top and use braces."r" let tokens = quote_spanned! {span=>"r"     Box::into_raw(Box::new(#init))"r" The lack of space before the `=>` should look jarring to Rust programmers"r" and this is intentional. The formatting is designed to be visibly"r" off-balance and draw the eye a particular way, due to the span expression"r" being evaluated in the context of the procedural macro and the remaining"r" tokens being evaluated in the generated code."r" `ToTokens` implementation. Tokens that originate within the `quote_spanned!`"r" invocation are spanned with the given span argument."r" The following procedural macro code uses `quote_spanned!` to assert that a"r" particular Rust type implements the [`Sync`] trait so that references can be"r" safely shared between threads."r" # use quote::{quote_spanned, TokenStreamExt, ToTokens};"r" # use proc_macro2::{Span, TokenStream};"r" # struct Type;"r" # impl Type {"r" #     fn span(&self) -> Span {"r" #         Span::call_site()"r" # impl ToTokens for Type {"r" #     fn to_tokens(&self, _tokens: &mut TokenStream) {}"r" # let ty = Type;"r" # let call_site = Span::call_site();"r" let ty_span = ty.span();"r" let assert_sync = quote_spanned! {ty_span=>"r"     struct _AssertSync where #ty: Sync;"r" If the assertion fails, the user will see an error like the following. The"r" input span of their type is highlighted in the error."r" ```text"r" error[E0277]: the trait bound `*const (): std::marker::Sync` is not satisfied"r"   --> src/main.rs:10:21"r"    |"r" 10 |     static ref PTR: *const () = &();"r"    |                     ^^^^^^^^^ `*const ()` cannot be shared between threads safely"r" In this example it is important for the where-clause to be spanned with the"r" line/column information of the user's input type so that error messages are"r" placed appropriately by the compiler."pounded_var_names// Extract the names of all #metavariables and pass them to the $call macro.// in:   pounded_var_names!(then!(...) a #b c #( #d )* #e)// out:  then!(... b);//       then!(... d);//       then!(... e);pounded_var_names_with_contextpounded_var_with_contextquote_bind_into_iterquote_bind_next_or_breakquote_each_token// The obvious way to write this macro is as a tt muncher. This implementation// does something more complex for two reasons.//   - With a tt muncher it's easy to hit Rust's built-in recursion_limit, which//     this implementation avoids because it isn't tail recursive.//   - Compile times for a tt muncher are quadratic relative to the length of//     the input. This implementation is linear, so it will be faster//     (potentially much faster) for big inputs. However, the constant factors//     of this implementation are higher than that of a tt muncher, so it is//     somewhat slower than a tt muncher if there are many invocations with//     short inputs.// An invocation like this://     quote_each_token!(_s a b c d e f g h i j);// expands to this://     quote_tokens_with_context!(_s//         (@  @  @  @   @   @   a   b   c   d   e   f   g  h  i  j)//         (@  @  @  @   @   a   b   c   d   e   f   g   h  i  j  @)//         (@  @  @  @   a   b   c   d   e   f   g   h   i  j  @  @)//         (@  @  @ (a) (b) (c) (d) (e) (f) (g) (h) (i) (j) @  @  @)//         (@  @  a  b   c   d   e   f   g   h   i   j   @  @  @  @)//         (@  a  b  c   d   e   f   g   h   i   j   @   @  @  @  @)//         (a  b  c  d