ies we handle UB on fishy arguments/// given to SmallVec. Draining and extending the allocation are fairly well-tested earlier, but/// `smallvec.insert(usize::MAX, val)` once slipped by!/// All code that indexes into SmallVecs should be tested with such "trivially wrong" args.max_removemax_swap_removetest_insert_out_of_bounds// We heap allocate all these strings so that double frees will show up under valgrind.macMacroDelimiterast_structcheck_keyword_matchesr" An attribute, like `#[repr(transparent)]`."r" Rust has six types of attributes."r" - Outer attributes like `#[repr(transparent)]`. These appear outside or"r"   in front of the item they describe."r" - Inner attributes like `#![feature(proc_macro)]`. These appear inside"r"   of the item they describe, usually a module."r" - Outer one-line doc comments like `/// Example`."r" - Inner one-line doc comments like `//! Please file an issue`."r" - Outer documentation blocks `/** Example */`."r" - Inner documentation blocks `/*! Please file an issue */`."r" The `style` field of type `AttrStyle` distinguishes whether an attribute"r" is outer or inner."r" Every attribute has a `path` that indicates the intended interpretation"r" of the rest of the attribute's contents. The path and the optional"r" additional contents are represented together in the `meta` field of the"r" attribute in three possible varieties:"r" - Meta::Path &mdash; attributes whose information content conveys just a"r"   path, for example the `#[test]` attribute."r" - Meta::List &mdash; attributes that carry arbitrary tokens after the"r"   path, surrounded by a delimiter (parenthesis, bracket, or brace). For"r"   example `#[derive(Copy)]` or `#[precondition(x < 5)]`."r" - Meta::NameValue &mdash; attributes with an `=` sign after the path,"r"   followed by a Rust expression. For example `#[path ="r#"   "sys/windows.rs"]`."#r" All doc comments are represented in the NameValue style with a path of"r#" "doc", as this is how they are processed by the compiler and by"#r" `macro_rules!` macros."r" #[derive(Copy, Clone)]"r"   ~~~~~~Path"r"   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^Meta::List"r#" #[path = "sys/windows.rs"]"#r"   ~~~~Path"r"   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^Meta::NameValue"r" #[test]"r"   ^^^^Meta::Path"r" # Parsing from tokens to Attribute"r" This type does not implement the [`Parse`] trait and thus cannot be"r" parsed directly by [`ParseStream::parse`]. Instead use"r" [`ParseStream::call`] with one of the two parser functions"r" [`Attribute::parse_outer`] or [`Attribute::parse_inner`] depending on"r" which you intend to parse."r" [`Parse`]: crate::parse::Parse"r" [`ParseStream::parse`]: crate::parse::ParseBuffer::parse"r" [`ParseStream::call`]: crate::parse::ParseBuffer::call"r" use syn::{Attribute, Ident, Result, Token};"r" use syn::parse::{Parse, ParseStream};"r" // Parses a unit struct with attributes."r" //"r#" //     #[path = "s.tmpl"]"#r" //     struct S;"r" struct UnitStruct {"r"     attrs: Vec<Attribute>,"r"     struct_token: Token![struct],"r"     name: Ident,"r"     semi_token: Token![;],"r" impl Parse for UnitStruct {"r"     fn parse(input: ParseStream) -> Result<Self> {"r"         Ok(UnitStruct {"r"             attrs: input.call(Attribute::parse_outer)?,"r"             struct_token: input.parse()?,"r"             name: input.parse()?,"r"             semi_token: input.parse()?,"r"         })"r" # Parsing from Attribute to structured arguments"r" The grammar of attributes in Rust is very flexible, which makes the"r" syntax tree not that useful on its own. In particular, arguments of the"r" `Meta::List` variety of attribute are held in an arbitrary `tokens:"r" TokenStream`. Macros are expected to check the `path` of the attribute,"r" decide whether they recognize it, and then parse the remaining tokens"r" according to whatever grammar they wish to require for that kind of"r" attribute. Use [`parse_args()`] to parse those tokens into the expected"r" data structure."r" [`parse_args()`]: Attribute::parse_args"r" # Doc comments"r" The compiler transforms doc comments, such as `/// comment` and `/*!"r" comment */`, into attributes before macros are expanded. Each comment is"r#" expanded into an attribute of the form `#[doc = r"comment"]`."#r" As an example, the following `mod` items are expanded identically:"r" # use syn::{ItemMod, parse_quote};"r" let doc: ItemMod = parse_quote! {"r"     /// Single line doc comments"r"     /// We write so many!"r"     /**"r"      * Multi-line comments..."r"      * May span many lines"r"      */"r"     mod example {"r"         //! Of course, they can be inner too"r"         /*! And fit in a single line */"r" let attr: ItemMod = parse_quote! {"r#"     #[doc = r" Single line doc comments"]"#r#"     #[doc = r" We write so many!"]"#r#"     #[doc = r""#r#"      "]"#r#"         #![doc = r" Of course, they can be inner too"]"#r#"         #![doc = r" And fit in a single line "]"#r" assert_eq!(doc, attr);"pound_tokenPoundAttrStylebracket_token/// Returns the path that identifies the interpretation of this attribute./// For example this would return the `test` in `#[test]`, the `derive` in/// `#[derive(Copy)]`, and the `path` in `#[path = "sys/windows.rs"]`.parse_args/// Parse the arguments to the attribute as a syntax tree./// This is similar to pulling out the `TokenStream` from `Meta::List` and/// doing `syn::parse2::<T>(meta_list.tokens)`, except that using/// `parse_args` the error message has a more useful span when `tokens` is/// empty./// The surrounding delimiters are *not* included in the input to the/// parser./// #[my_attr(value < 5)]///           ^^^^^^^^^ what gets parsed/// use syn::{parse_quote, Attribute, Expr};/// let attr: Attribute = parse_quote! {///     #[precondition(value < 5)]/// if attr.path().is_ident("precondition") {///     let precondition: Expr = attr.parse_args()?;/// # anyhow::Ok(())parse_args_with/// Parse the arguments to the attribute using the given parser./// use syn::{parse_quote, Attribute};///     #[inception { #[brrrrrrraaaaa