e_peek("21"), Ok(("", "21")));/// assert_eq!(parser.parse_peek("a21c"), Ok(("a21c", "")));/// assert_eq!(digit0::<_, ErrMode<ContextError>>.parse_peek(Partial::new("21c")), Ok((Partial::new("c"), "21")));/// assert_eq!(digit0::<_, ErrMode<ContextError>>.parse_peek(Partial::new("a21c")), Ok((Partial::new("a21c"), "")));/// assert_eq!(digit0::<_, ErrMode<ContextError>>.parse_peek(Partial::new("")), Err(ErrMode::Incomplete(Needed::new(1))));digit1/// Recognizes one or more ASCII numerical characters: `'0'..='9'`/// pub fn digit1<'i>(input: &mut &'i str) -> ModalResult<&'i str>/// #     winnow::ascii::digit1.parse_next(input)/// # use winnow::ascii::digit1;///     digit1.parse_next(input)/// assert!(parser.parse_peek("c1").is_err());/// assert_eq!(digit1::<_, ErrMode<ContextError>>.parse_peek(Partial::new("21c")), Ok((Partial::new("c"), "21")));/// assert!(digit1::<_, ErrMode<ContextError>>.parse_peek(Partial::new("c1")).is_err());/// assert_eq!(digit1::<_, ErrMode<ContextError>>.parse_peek(Partial::new("")), Err(ErrMode::Incomplete(Needed::new(1))));/// ## Parsing an integer/// You can use `digit1` in combination with [`Parser::try_map`] to parse an integer:/// fn parser<'s>(input: &mut &'s str) -> ModalResult<u32> {///   digit1.try_map(str::parse).parse_next(input)/// assert_eq!(parser.parse_peek("416"), Ok(("", 416)));/// assert_eq!(parser.parse_peek("12b"), Ok(("b", 12)));/// assert!(parser.parse_peek("b").is_err());hex_digit0/// Recognizes zero or more ASCII hexadecimal numerical characters: `'0'..='9'`, `'A'..='F'`,/// `'a'..='f'`/// *Complete version*: Will return the whole input if no terminating token is found (a non hexadecimal digit character)./// or if no terminating token is found (a non hexadecimal digit character)./// pub fn hex_digit0<'i>(input: &mut &'i str) -> ModalResult<&'i str>/// #     winnow::ascii::hex_digit0.parse_next(input)/// # use winnow::ascii::hex_digit0;///     hex_digit0.parse_next(input)/// assert_eq!(parser.parse_peek("21cZ"), Ok(("Z",